ef 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544780 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544780 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544780 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544780 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544780 #edb/ref 1089 #edb/ref 17592186544781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544780 #edb/ref 1090 #uuid "5f50aa9a-2389-5024-b199-8e25913107a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544781 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186544898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544781 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186544899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544781 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186544932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544781 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186544934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544781 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186544940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544781 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186544953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544781 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186544955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544781 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186544959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544781 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186544961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544781 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186544979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544781 #edb/ref 1085 "![](images/49/49ead9d9cf872e2e5086ec6a7b2c612b70443e0275d65e969215271478d24d36.png)\n\nConsider the polar curve $r=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544781 #edb/ref 1086 #uuid "f2d248c5-c3a9-5d9c-bcfc-f830bcac9594" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544781 #edb/ref 1087 #edb/ref 17592186544955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544782 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544782 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133228" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544782 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544782 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the slope of the tangent line to the given polar curve at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nSlope $=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544782 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "00bb583b-38c4-5b10-945e-a15928b12671" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544782 #edb/ref 1074 "In parts 2 and 3, we found that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nNow, to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /3} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}{{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{(-3)} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544784 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544784 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544784 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133225" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544784 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544784 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $x(\\theta )$ and $y(\\theta )$ represent the $x$- and $y$-coordinates of a point on the given curve. Write expressions for $x(\\theta )$ and $y(\\theta )$ in terms of $\\theta .$\n\n$x(\\theta )\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$y(\\theta )\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544784 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "04c21b11-d6eb-5e2f-9a68-02bdca097cfd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544784 #edb/ref 1074 "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use \n\n> $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ and $\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$\n\nwhere $x,$ $y,$ and $r$ are functions of $\\theta .$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we get \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\ny=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =6{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544798 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544798 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133226" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544798 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544798 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the derivative of $y(\\theta )=6{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $ with respect to $\\theta .$\n\n${y}^{′}(\\theta )\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544798 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2749929e-e01c-5706-82d9-161e96c84d4c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544798 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write\n\n$$\ny(\\theta )=6(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $y(\\theta )$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=6{u}^{2},\\,u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=12u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}\\theta } \\\\  & =12u\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544803 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544803 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133227" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544803 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544803 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the derivative of $x(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,$  with respect to $\\theta .$\n\n${x}^{′}(\\theta )\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544803 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "39c6a843-fd29-52a1-8f7b-ab703e4af195" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544803 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write our function as the composite function\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,u=2\\theta .\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}u}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}\\theta } \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544821 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544821 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133231" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544821 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544821 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent line to our polar curve at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nThe tangent line is $y\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544821 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5bcca525-fc41-52cc-be8b-f245ac9162a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544821 #edb/ref 1074 "Previously, we found that the slope of the tangent line at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is\n\n$$\nm=-\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\ny-{y}_{0}=m(x-{x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ are the coordinates of the point on the curve that correspond to $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Let's work these out. \n\nIn part 1, we found that\n\n$\\,$ $x(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,$ and $\\,y(\\theta )=6{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{0} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\\\ {y}_{0} & =6{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})=(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{9}{2}).$ \n\nFinally, the equation of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{0} & =m(x-{x}_{0}) \\\\ y-\\frac{9}{2} & =-\\sqrt{3}(x-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ y-\\frac{9}{2} & =-\\sqrt{3}x+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ y & =-\\sqrt{3}x+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/b8/b8546c0b6c51577acf25587fc1ebfc88e15e5f3b9456a64c499d640851bb44d0.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544829 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544829 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544829 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544829 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133232" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544829 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544829 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f6/f6cac98f2c8c7cf182ef5cd88019c12a8b8c6ad8e52d0a01f8174332b366eb7e.png)\n\nThe upper half of our polar curve can be expressed in Cartesian coordinates as $y=\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}+3.$ Write an integral expression that gives the exact area $A$ of the region enclosed between the curve and the  $x$-axis, as shown above.\n\n$A={\\int }_{a}^{b}$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{d}x,\\,\\,$ where  \n$a\\,=\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,b\\,=\\,$\n{{field-3}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544829 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "68d6c745-8c8f-5b38-826d-b15655a70429" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544829 #edb/ref 1074 "We have the curve\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}+3\n$$\n\nand the limits of integration $x=-3$ and $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the required area is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}+3\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544842 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544842 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133233" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544842 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544842 #edb/ref 1071 "Given $r(\\theta )=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ find the third derivative of $r(\\theta )$ with respect to $\\theta .$\n\n${r}^{(3)}(\\theta )\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544842 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "88c04c0e-97a3-5196-b229-a35ba120f617" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544842 #edb/ref 1074 "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(\\theta ) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{(3)}(\\theta ) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544853 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544853 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133229" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544853 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544853 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for our polar curve.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544853 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9ab9373f-f442-5c71-ba1a-50adb5530bb2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544853 #edb/ref 1074 "In parts 2 and 3, we found that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nNow, to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544858 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544858 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133230" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544858 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544858 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for our polar curve.\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544858 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9dac1de0-3399-5e2c-82ff-248d3a51fed2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544858 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta })}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPreviously, we've found that\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).$ \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx}) & =\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta )) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2{sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2{sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544864 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544864 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133234" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544864 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544864 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $r(\\theta )=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ and let ${P}_{2}(\\theta )$ be the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $r(\\theta )$ about $\\theta =0.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when ${P}_{2}(\\theta )$ is used to approximate $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.2).$ Give your answer to three decimal places.\n\n$|{R}_{2}|\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544864 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "acc186f8-9dad-51c7-bad3-42fb2258f011" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544864 #edb/ref 1074 "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when using the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}$ about $\\theta =a$ to approximate $r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{M}{3!}|\\theta -a{|}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{r}^{(3)}(\\theta )|$ on the interval between $a=0$ and $\\theta =0.2.$\n\nPlugging $\\theta =0.2$ and $a=0$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{6}|0.2{|}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to maximize $|{r}^{(3)}(\\theta )|$ on the interval $[0,0.2].$ Previously, we found that\n\n$$\n{r}^{(3)}(\\theta )=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that for $\\theta \\in [0,0.2],$ we have\n\n$$\n|{r}^{(3)}(\\theta )|=|-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta |=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $|{r}^{(3)}(\\theta )|=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is a decreasing function on the interval $[0,0.2],$ its value is greatest at $\\theta =0.$ So,\n\n$$\nM=|{r}^{(3)}(0)|=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{6}{6}|0.2{|}^{3} \\\\  & =(0.2)^{3} \\\\  & =0.008.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544898 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186544979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544898 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "01f21eb4-b1a0-59f4-8a66-c8d33fde667e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544898 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186544782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544899 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186544959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544899 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "02dd2953-b049-5f76-b85b-d1d16110fb37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544899 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186544829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544932 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186544934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544932 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "62c92e04-2b19-5e33-acac-324e656f0308" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544932 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186544858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544934 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186544899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544934 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "652fe64c-4218-5234-bbba-8607c50ad3db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544934 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186544821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544938 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6fa7716a-64b5-5197-92fc-82bb7b39f774" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544938 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186544780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544940 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7839cf03-a167-5954-9d88-64cb7b07f6e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544940 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186544864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544953 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186544898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544953 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "92c23f02-c867-5293-85b3-97e296860214" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544953 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186544803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544955 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186544961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544955 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9b23ac8e-4657-52ad-ab92-2f6c1ebcf754" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544955 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186544784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544959 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186544940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544959 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid 